it's been quite a while since i've done  a trolley problem memes video  and i thought it would be kind of cool  to get back to it and to focus on  some of the great memes that are out  there that tie  in directly with philosophy  so there's there's quite a few out there  i picked the top ten  we should remind ourselves what the  trolley problem is  there's two main formulations that you  can see  actually there's lots and lots of other  formulations but the first one  has a trolley and  a divergence in a track as you can see  here  if you let the trolley keep going on its  normal path it's going to crush and kill  five people and if you flip the switch  then one person will die in place of  five  another variation on this is to have a  fat man that you push  off of the bridge who will actually stop  the trolley  so once again sacrificing one person  to save five and the idea is  that how you approach this problem  shows whether you're more deontological  or more utilitarian  and there is no proper solution to the  thing  as a matter of fact there's an  anti-solution which many people have  proposed  called called multi-track drifting but  we won't talk about that  here so let's take a look at the memes  here's the first one i like this one  you're a podgy utilitarian you do not  yet have the sufficient mass to stop a  trolley but  with your current dietary habits you're  slowly getting there  every day on your way to work you have  to walk across a railway overpass  is it your obligation to eat enough to  obtain trolley stopping weight in case a  trolley problem one day  occurs and now you know a utilitarian of  course would say  no because there's other factors  involved but this is this is a great way  to  approach the problem and to think about  it in  utilitarian fashion so that's kind of a  funny one  the next one you know playing off of  albert camus you see  that that's his face right there saying  there's only one serious  philosophical trolley problem of course  that's a reference  to his his beginning of the myth of  sisyphus where he says that there's one  serious philosophical problem and that  is of  suicide of course he's going to change  that later on  and the rebel to murder as well  but you know you notice that it's a  question of whether  he flips the switch or doesn't and if he  doesn't  suppose i guess it runs over him  the next one has got two different  philosophers  and these are two social contract  theorists that we  often oppose to each other when we're  teaching them in intro to philosophy  classes or in textbooks  so the state of nature according to  hobbs or lock in hobbs  the trolley will continue towards b that  is on the path which isn't going to hurt  anybody  unless you were the stranger redirected  towards the other one well you hope the  stranger  doesn't pull the lever or will you do it  before he does presumably whoever pulls  the lever first gets it to go  towards the other person and hobbes is  of course very famous for having said  that in the state of nature we can't  trust each other  life is uh nasty brutish and short  and there's causes for human conflict  which which  even stem far from our own rationality  the lockheed trolley problem instead in  the state of nature were actually quite  good to each other  the lot the trolley will continue  towards b everything's fine  there's no flips or levers to flip  or anything like that uh it's a bit of a  misrepresentation i would say  since the state of nature is a little  bit more complicated than that but it's  a good meme  nonetheless here's one that's even  funnier  so you know who that is right that's ein  rand  and she's got the trolley and she's  definitely going to flip the switch  to protect one's beloved husband now of  course she doesn't doesn't have one but  a lover it could be right versus a  thousand strangers this is coming from  her discussion  in the um the virtue of selfishness  when she talks about how how it is that  you could sacrifice yourself  or sacrifice other things for a beloved  person  and in this case you'd sacrifice any  unlimited number of people who you  really don't care much about  the next one this is a really good one i  may actually use this in class the veil  of ignorance  the trolley problem so veil of ignorance  is coming from  john rawls a theory of justice and the  idea is that when we're like  reconstituting a society  as essentially as a thought experiment  we do so behind a veil of ignorance we  have no  idea where we're going to end up in the  society so the idea is that we'd make  the society a bit more  equitable a bit more just we'd limit  people's exposure to  you know things that make them  vulnerable for irrational reasons so in  this case you don't know where you're  going to be  in the trolley problem but you have to  choose the scenario  in advance but of course the only the  only choice that they give you here is  do you want the lever  to be pulled and i think the rational  thing to do  in that case would be in fact to pull  the lever  although you could say well you don't  know whether you're going to be  more utilitarian or made more  deontological with this so  maybe maybe there's no solution  actually possible in in this  case not quite quite sure  now we get into the versions of the  trolley  problem where bringing philosophers in  is  actually turning it into a different  kind of  kind of problem or issue and so we've  got  zeno here right this is of course zeno  of aleah  and the trolley problem is in this case  turned into the fact that you could  never actually crush anybody  because if you remember your your basic  you know pre-socratic philosophy xeno  had said well you gotta cross the  halfway point  and then you gotta get to the halfway  point between that and the object and  then halfway point of that and halfway  so you never  actually complete completed these were  arguments that zeno was giving to  support his uh  his uh teacher permanity's contention  that that  change in motion was impossible so  that's really kind of a different  trolley problem  and we can say something similar as well  with this interesting variation  as well the chinese room trolley problem  of course coming from john searle's  chinese room  issues a man who does not understand  philosophy is enclosed within a room  he's continually fed trolley problems on  slips of paper  he answers them all according to a fixed  set of instructions inside the room  he manages to convince some of the  people outside the room he's really a  philosopher  however does he really understand  philosophy  so this is analogous to a problem about  you know artificial intelligence and  whether whether  a computer could be said to really  understand  um and notice that what it picks in the  end  its output is always multi-track  drifting which is let's kill  everybody so that's kind of an  interesting one  here is the the last of these  problems the ship of theseus trolley  problem  this is changing things considerably if  you do not pull the lever one person  will be crushed to death instantly  if you do pull the lever the trolley  will divert onto a thousand mile stretch  of track with one person tied down at  the end of it so somebody's getting  killed no matter what  if as the trolley rolls down this  thousand mile track a crew  systematically switches out every piece  of the original trolley  with a replacement part did the trolley  which you diverted  kill the man so this is changed from a  moral problem  into a question about the identity  the metaphysical identity of the trolley  and  you know again very different kind of  problem here isn't it  here's another where the trolley problem  is being you could say  reimagined in a somewhat different way  so this is the arthur  schopenhauer trolley problem  schopenhauer of course  was you know pretty pessimistic about  life so let's just see what this one  says  according to schopenhauer's  anti-natalist view the sum of all human  experience  is overwhelmingly negative therefore  pulling the lever would only take one  person out of this dreaded existence and  cause the  five to continue living with horrible  pain associated with life  assuming this pessimistic philosophical  view with the utilitarian thing be  for the first time to not pull the lever  so the utilitarian thing mixing  utilitarianism and schopenhauer together  of course  which are not exactly the same and  schopenhauer by the way doesn't think  that everybody's aware  of how crappy life is so what you've  really got are a whole bunch of  schopenhauerns  tied to the tracks people who've had the  sort of  vision or insight that that he has  and it reminds me of the old joke about  masochists and sadists right the  masochist is the one who says hurt me  hurt me and the sadist is the one who  says no i won't  so this is kind of a funny one  now i saved a a final one this is  actually  coming from a cartoon and this is really  quite great  you notice you've got phyllis philosophy  convention sightseeing to her  and the guys saying everyone the trolley  is out of control you have three seconds  to bail out before we're going too fast  and it says by the time we realized it  wasn't a thought experiment it was too  late  which probably would be the case with  philosophers so  this is a a set of uh new trolley memes  well not really new  uh just new in this video some of these  have been floating around for quite a  while but i thought you might get a kick  out of them at least as much  as i have so i'll i may do more of these  in the future if i find some other  interesting  trolley memes to take a look at that i  haven't put into  this or the previous two trolley meme  videos but  for the for the time being that's all  folks  you
 

